/// A user's Google Workspace for an assistant, as native tools, each answered
/// by `api-request` calls against the `oauth_provider` the instance names,
/// stamped with the calling conversation's owner and compacted to what a model
/// needs. The user's token never reaches this component; `api-request`
/// attaches it host-side. Every call is nested under a request: an instance
/// whose clauses say `user = "*"` cannot call on its own account, so a caller
/// with no user of its own is answered `denied`.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// hosts, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tools exist; `log` records each
/// step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a query.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `gmail_host` is the `host[:port]` Gmail is called on
/// and `calendar_host` the one Calendar is called on, each of which
/// `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts` and the
/// instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails every
/// activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up to
/// a power of two. The config check holds Σ to at most 32
/// (`PROVIDER_CONCURRENCY_CEILING`), and 64 holds that twice over, so 64 is
/// the documented value: tools joining this class change nothing a deployed
/// inbox needs, and the family default of 16 is too small for a full window.
///
/// Every nested answer crosses the bus as a tool result, so Google's body — a
/// JSON string inside `api-request`'s value, inside the result envelope — must
/// fit `[messaging] max_body_bytes` (64 KiB default). Either raise that cap to
/// carry the OAuth provider's `max_response_bytes` (256 KiB default) with a
/// margin for escaping, `messaging { max_body_bytes = 393216; }`, or lower
/// `max_response_bytes` to fit the default cap, at the cost of most HTML mail.
/// Half again covers message bodies, which are base64url and do not grow when
/// escaped; sender-written headers dense in quotes or backslashes escape to
/// about twice their size, so such a read can pass the cap below the read
/// limit.
/// An answer over the bus cap never reaches this component as Google's answer;
/// the tools treat that refusal as they treat an answer cut at the read limit.
component GoogleWorkspaceTools {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide gmail-labels {
    mcp_name = "GmailLabels";
    description = "The user's Gmail labels. Without `id`, every label with its `id`, `name` and `type` (`system` or `user`), in Google's order; Google gives no counts in a listing. With `id` (a label's `id`, such as `INBOX` or `Label_12`), that one label with its `messages_total`, `messages_unread`, `threads_total` and `threads_unread`, each when Google gives it. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-search {
    mcp_name = "GmailSearch";
    description = "Search the user's Gmail with Gmail's search syntax (`q`, as in Gmail's search box: `from:alice@example.com is:unread newer_than:7d`). Answers `messages`, newest first as Google orders them, one line per message: `id`, `thread_id`, `from`, `subject` and `date` (the header values as sent, each when present; a value over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) is cut, ends in `…`, and the line's `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `snippet` (Gmail's preview of the text) and `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` and `UNREAD` by id); `result_size_estimate` (Google's estimate of all matches); `next_page` when there are more — pass it back as `page` with the same `q`; and `unavailable`, the listed messages that could not be read, each `id` and `reason`: deleted before it could be read, or its read was cut at the provider's read limit or refused by the host (a reason naming neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault: searching again may read it); the page's other messages are answered. `max` is how many messages to return, 1 to 25, default 10; each costs one call to Google. `spam_trash: true` searches spam and trash too. Bodies are not included. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "q": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1024, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 25 }, "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "spam_trash": { "type": "boolean" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["q"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide gmail-read {
    mcp_name = "GmailRead";
    description = "Read one Gmail message by `id` (a message `id` from `GmailSearch`). Answers `id`, `thread_id`, `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` by id), the headers `from`, `to`, `cc`, `bcc`, `reply_to`, `subject`, `date`, `message_id` and `in_reply_to` (each as sent, when present; `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `reply_to` are cut over 4000 bytes (UTF-8), the other headers over 1000; a cut value ends in `…` and `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `text` (the body as text: of a plain and an HTML alternative the plain one, parts of a mixed message joined by a blank line; HTML is read as its text, content it hides included — tags, styles and scripts dropped, a link's text kept without its address, table cells separated by tabs), `text_source` (`text/plain`, `text/html`, `mixed`, or `none` when the message has no text part), `text_chars` (the whole text's length in characters), `attachments` (each `filename`, `mime_type`, `size` in bytes and `inline` — shown in the body rather than attached; an attachment's `filename` or `mime_type` over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) is cut and ends in `…`), and `decoding_notes` when something could not be decoded exactly (an unsupported charset, invalid bytes). `max_chars` (200 to 60000, default 12000) bounds `text`: `text` is cut at `max_chars` characters, and shorter when the whole answer would otherwise exceed the wrapper's result ceiling; `text_truncated` and `text_chars` say so either way. A message too large to read whole — larger than the provider's read limit, or refused by the host — is answered without its body: `text` is null, `text_omitted` says why, and `snippet` (Gmail's preview) stands in for it; a reason naming neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, and reading it again may answer the body. To read a thread, read its messages one by one. Attachment contents are not fetched. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "max_chars": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 200, "maximum": 60000 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide gmail-thread {
    mcp_name = "GmailThread";
    description = "One Gmail thread's messages by `id` (a `thread_id` from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`), oldest first, without bodies. Answers `id`, `subject` (the first message's), `message_count` (how many messages the thread holds), `messages_omitted` when any are left out (`messages_omitted` counts the messages left out — older than the newest `max`, or past the wrapper's result ceiling), and `messages`, each with `id`, `from`, `to`, `cc`, `subject` (only when it differs from the thread's), `date` (the header values as sent, each when present; `to` and `cc` are cut over 4000 bytes (UTF-8), the other headers over 1000; a cut value ends in `…` and `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut — on the message, or on the thread for the thread's `subject`), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `snippet` (Gmail's preview of the text) and `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` by id). `max` is how many of the newest messages to return, 1 to 100, default 30. The whole thread is read before `max` applies. A thread too large to read whole is read again without `to` and `cc`, and `recipients_omitted` says why; a thread whose `from`, `subject` and `date` alone are too large is refused as `internal` (naming the provider's `max_response_bytes` or the bus's `max_body_bytes`), and a smaller `max` does not help. A `recipients_omitted` or `internal` reason naming neither is a transient fault: reading the thread again may answer it whole. Read a message's text with `GmailRead`. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 100 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide calendar-list {
    mcp_name = "CalendarList";
    description = "The calendars in the user's Google Calendar list, in Google's order. Answers `calendars`, each with `id`, `summary` (the calendar's name; over 300 bytes (UTF-8) it is cut, ends in `…`, and the entry's `fields_truncated` lists it), `primary: true` on the user's own calendar, `access_role` (`owner`, `writer`, `reader` or `freeBusyReader`) and `time_zone`; and `next_page` when there are more than 100 — pass it back as `page`. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide calendar-events {
    mcp_name = "CalendarEvents";
    description = "The events on one of the user's Google calendars in the window `from` to `to` (each RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; Google refuses a time without one, and a `to` not after `from`), recurring events expanded into their occurrences, in start order. `calendar` is a calendar `id` from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar). Answers `events`, each with `id`, `summary`, `start` and `end` (`{date_time, time_zone?}` for a timed event, `{date}` for an all-day one, as Google gives them; nothing is converted), `location`, `status` (`confirmed`, `tentative` or `cancelled`), `organizer` (the organizer's email) and `self_response` (the answer to the invitation of the calendar read — `needsAction`, `declined`, `tentative` or `accepted` — when that calendar is an attendee: on `primary` the user's own, on another person's calendar that person's; a declined event is on that calendar but is not a commitment); `summary` and `location` over 300 bytes (UTF-8) and `organizer` over 320 are cut, end in `…`, and the event's `fields_truncated` lists them; `time_zone` (the calendar's); and `next_page` when there are more — pass it back as `page` with the same `calendar`, `from`, `to` and `q`. Other attendees are not listed: `CalendarEvent` reads one event with its attendees. `q` searches the events' text. `max` is how many events to return, 1 to 50, default 50. A window too large to read whole is refused as `internal`; the refusal says what to narrow. A refusal whose reason names neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, not a window too large: asking again with the same arguments may read the window whole. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "from": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "to": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "q": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1024, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 50 }, "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["from", "to"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide calendar-event {
    mcp_name = "CalendarEvent";
    description = "One event of one of the user's Google calendars by `id` (an event `id` from `CalendarEvents`). `calendar` is the calendar `id` the event is on, from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar); an event on another calendar is not found without it. Answers `id`, `summary`, `description` (as the organizer wrote it, which may contain HTML; it is not decoded), `start` and `end` (`{date_time, time_zone?}` for a timed event, `{date}` for an all-day one, as Google gives them; nothing is converted), `location`, `status` (`confirmed`, `tentative` or `cancelled`), `organizer` (`email`, `name`, and `self: true` when the organizer is the calendar read), `self_response` (the answer to the invitation of the calendar read — `needsAction`, `declined`, `tentative` or `accepted` — when that calendar is an attendee; a declined event is on that calendar but is not a commitment), `attendees` (each `email`, `name`, `response` and `optional: true` for an optional guest), `attendees_total` and `attendee_responses` (how many attendees `accepted`, `declined`, `tentative` and `needs_action`), `recurrence` (the recurring event's rules, RRULE lines, at most 20; `recurrence_truncated` when there are more), `recurring_event_id` (the recurring event an occurrence belongs to) and `conference` (the video meeting link), each when present. The calendar read is the user on `primary` and another person on that person's calendar: there `organizer.self` and `self_response` are that person's, and the user's own response is the user's entry in `attendees`. An event with more than 50 attendees is answered with the calendar read's own entry alone and `attendees_omitted: true`; `attendees_total` and `attendee_responses` are given only when every attendee is listed. `summary`, `location`, the organizer's `name`, each `recurrence` line and `conference` over 1000 bytes (UTF-8), an attendee's `name` over 300, and an `email` over 320 are cut, end in `…`, and the object's `fields_truncated` lists them. `max_chars` (200 to 60000, default 12000) bounds `description`: it is cut at `max_chars` characters, and shorter when the whole answer would otherwise exceed the wrapper's result ceiling; `description_truncated` and `description_chars` (the whole description's length in characters) say so either way. An event too large to read whole is refused as `internal`, naming the provider's `max_response_bytes` or the bus's `max_body_bytes`; a refusal naming neither is a transient fault, and reading the event again may answer it. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,1024}$" }, "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "max_chars": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 200, "maximum": 60000 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide calendar-free-busy {
    mcp_name = "CalendarFreeBusy";
    description = "When the user's Google calendars are busy in the window `from` to `to` (each RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; Google refuses a time without one, and a `to` not after `from`). `calendars` is a list of 1 to 50 distinct calendar `id`s from `CalendarList`, default `[\"primary\"]` (the user's own calendar); the ids together may hold at most 48000 bytes of UTF-8, and more is refused: ask for fewer. Answers `from` and `to` (the window as Google read it) and `calendars`, keyed by each calendar `id` asked, each with `busy` (the busy intervals, each `start` and `end` as Google gives them; a time not in one is free) or `errors` (Google's reasons it could not answer for that calendar, such as `notFound`; the calendar's busy times are then unknown, not free). Event details are not included: read them with `CalendarEvents`. A window too large to read or answer whole is refused as `internal`; the refusal says what to narrow. A refusal that says the host did not deliver api-request's answer and names neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, not a window too large: asking again with the same arguments may read the window whole. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "from": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "to": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "calendars": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 50, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" } }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["from", "to"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-draft {
    mcp_name = "GmailDraft";
    description = "Create a draft in the user's Gmail. The draft is saved in Gmail's Drafts for the user to review and send from Gmail; no tool sends it. `to` and `cc` are the recipients as an email's header writes them (`Alice Example <alice@example.com>, bob@example.com`), each 1 to 980 printable ASCII characters: a display name in another script is left out or written in ASCII. `subject` is at most 500 characters with no control character or line break, and is required unless `reply_to_id` is given. `body` is the message's plain text, at most 20000 characters and about 20000 bytes of UTF-8 text, where a line break counts as two; a longer body is refused. `reply_to_id` (a message `id` from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`) makes the draft a reply in that message's thread: the original is read first, which needs the Gmail read scope set, and the draft carries the original's `Message-ID` as `In-Reply-To` and `References`. Without `subject`, a reply's subject is the original's with `Re: ` before it (kept as is when it already starts with `Re:`), and an original whose subject is over 1000 bytes is refused: give `subject`. Gmail files a draft in the thread only when its subject matches the thread's and it carries the original's `Message-ID`, so a `subject` given for a reply is the thread's. Answers `draft_id`, `message_id` and `thread_id` (when Google gives it); for a reply, also `in_reply_to` (the `Message-ID` the draft answers, absent when the original has no usable one), `subject` when it was derived, and `in_thread: false` when Gmail filed the draft outside the original's thread: the draft exists as a new conversation, so tell the user rather than drafting again; nothing the caller wrote is echoed. A refusal saying the draft may have been created means Google may have saved it before its answer was lost: look for it with `GmailSearch` (`q: in:drafts`) before asking again, or the user has two. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail compose scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "to": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,980}$" }, "cc": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,980}$" }, "subject": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 500, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "body": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 20000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "reply_to_id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["to", "body"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-modify-labels {
    mcp_name = "GmailModifyLabels";
    description = "Add labels to and remove labels from Gmail messages, in one change Google applies to every message or to none. `ids` is a list of 1 to 1000 distinct message `id`s from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`; the ids together may hold at most 48000 characters, and more is refused: ask for fewer. `add` and `remove` are each a list of 1 to 20 distinct label ids as `GmailLabels` lists them (`INBOX`, `UNREAD`, `Label_12`), never label names: at least one of them must be given, and no label may be in both. Removing `INBOX` archives a message; removing `UNREAD` marks it read. `add` may not name `TRASH` or `SPAM`: these tools never move mail to Trash or Spam, and such a change is refused; `remove` may name them. Answers `accepted: true`, `ids` (how many messages) and `add` and `remove` as asked: Google accepted the change for every message. A label or message id Google does not know is refused with Google's message, and nothing was changed. The change is idempotent: a refusal saying the labels may already have been changed means Google may have applied it before its answer was lost, and asking again with the same arguments is safe. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail organize scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "ids": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 1000, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "add": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 20, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "remove": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 20, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["ids"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide calendar-create {
    mcp_name = "CalendarCreate";
    description = "Create an event on one of the user's Google calendars. `calendar` is a calendar `id` from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar). `summary` (the event's title) is required; `summary` and `location` are at most 1000 characters each, `description` at most 2000. Google requires `start` and `end` of the same kind: both `YYYY-MM-DD` for an all-day event (`end` is the day after the last day), or both RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; nothing is converted. `time_zone` is an IANA name (`America/New_York`) and is required for a recurring timed event. `attendees` is a list of 1 to 50 distinct email addresses; together they may hold at most 8000 bytes of UTF-8, and more is refused. `recurrence` is 1 to 5 RRULE, RDATE or EXDATE lines as Google writes them (`RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO`), each 1 to 500 printable ASCII characters; with it the event is a recurring series, and `CalendarDelete` deletes the series only with `series: true`. `notify` is who Google emails about the event — `none` (default), `all`, or `externalOnly` (guests outside the user's domain); with attendees and `all`, Google sends invitations from the user's account. By default nobody is notified. Answers the event as `CalendarEvent` does, with its new `id`. A refusal saying the event may have been created means Google may have saved it before its answer was lost: look for it with `CalendarEvents` in its window before asking again, or the user has two. Conference links, reminders, attachments, colors and visibility are not arguments, and no tool moves an event to another calendar or clears a calendar. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar events scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "summary": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "start": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "end": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "description": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 2000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "location": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "attendees": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 50, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" } }, "time_zone": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "recurrence": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 5, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,500}$" } }, "notify": { "type": "string", "enum": ["none", "all", "externalOnly"] }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["summary", "start", "end"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 1;
  }
  provide calendar-update {
    mcp_name = "CalendarUpdate";
    description = "Change one event of one of the user's Google calendars by `id` (an event `id` from `CalendarEvents`). `calendar` is the calendar `id` the event is on, from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar). Changes only the fields named; a field not named keeps its value, and no field can be cleared (an `attendees` of `[]` removes every attendee). At least one of `summary`, `start` and `end`, `description`, `location`, `attendees` and `recurrence` must be given. `summary` and `location` are at most 1000 characters each, `description` at most 2000. `attendees` replaces the whole list: give the complete new list — `CalendarEvent` shows the current one. `attendees` is a list of 0 to 50 distinct email addresses; together they may hold at most 8000 bytes of UTF-8, and more is refused. If `start` or `end` is given, both must be, and `time_zone` only with them; they replace the event's time and may change it between all-day (`YYYY-MM-DD`) and timed (RFC 3339 with an offset). Both are of the same kind: both `YYYY-MM-DD` for an all-day event (`end` is the day after the last day), or both RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; nothing is converted. `time_zone` is an IANA name (`America/New_York`). `recurrence` is 1 to 5 RRULE, RDATE or EXDATE lines as Google writes them (`RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO`), each 1 to 500 printable ASCII characters, and replaces the event's rules; given with a timed `start`, it needs `time_zone`. `notify` is who Google emails about the change — `none` (default), `all`, or `externalOnly` (guests outside the user's domain). By default nobody is notified. Answers the event as Google holds it after the change, as `CalendarEvent` does. A refusal saying the change may already have been applied means Google may have applied it before its answer was lost: `CalendarEvent` shows the event as it is, and asking again with the same arguments is safe. Conference links, reminders, attachments, colors and visibility are not arguments, and no tool moves an event to another calendar or clears a calendar. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar events scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,1024}$" }, "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "summary": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "start": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "end": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "description": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 2000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "location": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "attendees": { "type": "array", "minItems": 0, "maxItems": 50, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" } }, "time_zone": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "recurrence": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 5, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,500}$" } }, "notify": { "type": "string", "enum": ["none", "all", "externalOnly"] }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 1;
  }
  provide calendar-delete {
    mcp_name = "CalendarDelete";
    description = "Deletes one event by `id` (an event `id` from `CalendarEvents`) from one of the user's Google calendars. `calendar` is the calendar `id` the event is on, from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar). The event is read first, which needs the same scope. An event that is a recurring series (it has `recurrence`) is deleted only with `series: true`, which removes every occurrence; without it the refusal names the event as a series. To delete one occurrence, give its own `id` from `CalendarEvents` (an occurrence's id carries a suffix) without `series`. An event already cancelled is refused as already deleted. `notify` is who Google emails about the cancellation — `none` (default), `all`, or `externalOnly`. By default nobody is notified. Answers `deleted: true`, `id`, the event's `summary` and `start` as read before deletion, and `series: true` for a series. A refusal saying the event may already be deleted means Google may have acted before its answer was lost: asking again is safe — a deleted event is refused as already cancelled. No tool moves an event to another calendar or clears a calendar. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar events scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,1024}$" }, "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "series": { "type": "boolean" }, "notify": { "type": "string", "enum": ["none", "all", "externalOnly"] }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 1;
  }
}
